# Mellomting accounting-log rotation policy (PLAN §42, FIX-11/N7).
#
# The daemon opens the JSONL once at startup and holds the file descriptor
# forever; it never reopens it. Rename-based rotation (logrotate's default)
# would therefore leave the daemon appending to the renamed inode while
# `mellomting usage report` reads an empty current file.
#
# copytruncate keeps the inode unchanged, so the daemon keeps appending to
# the same file that its Landlock policy granted at startup (PLAN §57) and
# no SIGHUP/reopen is involved. Do NOT switch to rename-based rotation under
# security.landlock.mode: required: a renamed file is a new inode the policy
# does not grant, and the daemon would silently keep writing to the rotated
# file until restart.
#
# copytruncate truncates in place; in the rare window between the copy and
# the truncate a line may be duplicated or lost, which is acceptable for
# token-accounting JSONL. If the daemon is stopped across rotation instead
# (e.g. systemctl stop && rotate && start), the default rename mode works.
#
# The daemon creates the log as 0640 owned by the service account; keep
# the `su` directive in sync with that account (create is not used with
# copytruncate, which truncates the existing file in place).
#
# Install as:  /etc/logrotate.d/mellomting
/var/log/mellomting/usage.jsonl {
    daily
    rotate 14
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
    su mellomting mellomting
}
